ic  is taking a breather but the damage may  already be done for many who would hope  to get in eventually polling by ipsos  exclusively for global news shows more  than 60 percent of respondents hoping to  get into the market are now giving up  it's going to continue to percolate as  housing prices rise inflation continues  and interest rates go up people in bc  and ontario home to the least affordable  housing markets feel most resigned to  renting or living with their parents the  dream hasn't been dashed everywhere  though in saskatchewan manitoba and the  atlantic provinces it lives on for the  majority  there are generational divides too  respondents under 35 say owning a home  is less important now than it was a  quarter century ago a sentiment not  shared as widely by older generations  and opinions differ when it comes to  outside the box solutions to home  ownership  younger canadians are most likely to be  open to co-ownership with family and  friends with three out of every four gen  z respondents and nearly 60 percent of  millennials saying their game to  purchase with others a lot of people are  looking at the home ownership question  in a new way as you said considering  other alternatives like buying a home  with somebody else or a partnership or  even just putting their money somewhere  else the federal budget pledged 10  billion dollars to help boost the supply  of affordable housing over the next five  years there's also a tax rebate for  first-time homebuyers to a maximum of  forty thousand dollars which doesn't go  far in markets like toronto and  vancouver seventy-five percent of  respondents say these measures won't  make enough of a difference and  economists agree in terms of  ownership costs with interest rates  rising things are going to get even more  difficult ann gaviola global news  toronto  18 well-earned wins and counting where  canadian matteo roach ranks among  jeopardy's greatest next  [Music]  reigning jeopardy champ mateo roach 